CAT = "capswRights"; SEG = "RIG"
# VLO 4 (person-centred, ethical, culturally safe care; consent) and VLO 12 (abuse and neglect).
Q = [
("Under the Health Care Consent Act, which is required for valid consent?",
 "It must be informed and voluntary", ["It must always be in writing", "It must be given by family", "It must be witnessed by two staff"],
 "Valid consent must relate to the treatment, be informed, be given voluntarily and not be obtained by misrepresentation or fraud. It can be spoken or implied as well as written."),
("Under the Health Care Consent Act, how is a person's capacity viewed?",
 "Presumed capable", ["Presumed incapable at 80", "Decided once for life", "Set by the closest relative"],
 "The Act presumes people are capable. Capacity is specific to each decision and time, so someone may be able to decide about a bath but not about surgery."),
("Who is highest on the Health Care Consent Act list of substitute decision-makers?",
 "A guardian of the person", ["The person's spouse or partner", "An adult child", "The Public Guardian and Trustee"],
 "The ranking starts with a guardian of the person, then an attorney for personal care, a representative appointed by the Consent and Capacity Board, then spouse or partner, children or parents, siblings and other relatives. The Public Guardian and Trustee is the last resort."),
("Which document names someone to make health care decisions if a person becomes incapable?",
 "Power of attorney for personal care", ["Continuing power of attorney for property", "A last will and testament", "A do not resuscitate form"],
 "A power of attorney for personal care, made under the Substitute Decisions Act, 1992, covers health care, shelter and similar decisions. A continuing power of attorney for property covers money and property."),
("How must a substitute decision-maker decide for an incapable person?",
 "Follow their known wishes", ["Do what the family prefers", "Do whatever staff recommend", "Choose the least costly option"],
 "A substitute decision-maker must follow the person's known wishes expressed while capable (at age 16 or older), and if none apply, act in the person's best interests."),
("A capable client refuses a shower today. What should you do?",
 "Respect it and report it", ["Give the shower anyway", "Ask the family to insist on it", "Skip it and chart it as given"],
 "A capable person may refuse care. Find out why, offer another time or a different option, and report and record the refusal. Forcing care can be abuse."),
("What is culturally safe care?",
 "Care the client feels is respectful", ["Care that treats everyone the same", "Care shaped by the PSW's own culture", "Care that avoids talking about culture"],
 "Cultural safety is judged by the person receiving care: they feel their identity, beliefs and practices are respected. Treating everyone the same can ignore real differences."),
("A resident wants to eat only halal food. What should you do?",
 "Pass the request to the team", ["Tell her the menu is fixed", "Remove the pork and serve the rest", "Ask her family to bring her own food"],
 "Religious and cultural food needs are part of person-centred care and the plan of care. Removing pork from a dish does not make it halal."),
("Which law contains the Residents' Bill of Rights for long-term care homes?",
 "Fixing Long-Term Care Act, 2021", ["Retirement Homes Act, 2010", "Health Care Consent Act, 1996", "Ontario Human Rights Code, 1990"],
 "The Fixing Long-Term Care Act, 2021 sets out the Residents' Bill of Rights, including the right to be treated with dignity and respect and to be free from abuse and neglect."),
("A resident in long-term care wants to keep her own photos and furniture in her room. This reflects which right?",
 "Keeping personal possessions", ["Managing her own medication", "Choosing her own care staff", "Refusing all room safety inspections"],
 "The Residents' Bill of Rights includes keeping and displaying personal possessions, pictures and furnishings in the room, within safety and other residents' rights."),
("Which action best protects a client's dignity during personal care?",
 "Knock and keep them covered", ["Leave the door open in case of a fall", "Talk with coworkers over them", "Hurry to finish quickly"],
 "Knocking, closing doors or curtains and exposing only the part being washed preserves privacy and dignity. Talking over the client treats them as an object."),
("Which is an example of financial abuse?",
 "Using a client's bank card for yourself", ["Helping a client pay a bill", "Keeping receipts for groceries bought", "Reporting missing money"],
 "Financial abuse is misusing a person's money or property. Helping with bills and keeping receipts, as directed by the plan of care, is proper practice."),
("Which is an example of neglect?",
 "Leaving a client soiled for hours", ["Offering a client a choice of meals", "Letting a client rest in bed after lunch", "Encouraging a client to walk"],
 "Neglect is failing to provide the care, help or services a person needs for health, safety or well-being. Leaving someone soiled risks skin breakdown and infection."),
("Which is an example of emotional abuse?",
 "Threatening a client", ["Gently redirecting a client", "Reminding a client about lunch", "Asking a client's preferences"],
 "Threats, insults, humiliation and isolation are emotional abuse. Redirecting and reminding are supportive approaches."),
("In a long-term care home, you suspect a resident is being abused by a staff member. Under the Fixing Long-Term Care Act, what must you do?",
 "Report it at once to the Director", ["Wait until you have proof", "Confront the staff member first", "Tell only the resident's family members"],
 "Anyone with reasonable grounds to suspect abuse or neglect of a resident that caused or risks harm must immediately report it to the Director at the Ministry of Long-Term Care, as well as follow the home's policy. Proof is not required."),
("Under the Retirement Homes Act, 2010, who receives mandatory reports of suspected harm to a resident?",
 "The Registrar of the RHRA", ["The local police chief", "The resident's family doctor", "The Ministry of Labour"],
 "The Retirement Homes Act requires reports of suspected abuse, neglect or improper care that caused or risks harm to go to the Registrar of the Retirement Homes Regulatory Authority."),
("Can a staff member be punished for reporting suspected abuse in good faith in long-term care?",
 "No, reporters are protected", ["Yes, if the report is unproven", "Yes, if it embarrasses the home", "Only if the union agrees"],
 "The Fixing Long-Term Care Act protects people who report in good faith from retaliation. This is so fear does not stop anyone reporting."),
("A child in a home where you give care tells you a parent hits them. What law sets your duty to report?",
 "Child, Youth and Family Services Act", ["Fixing Long-Term Care Act, 2021", "Retirement Homes Act, 2010", "Occupational Health and Safety Act"],
 "Ontario's Child, Youth and Family Services Act, 2017 requires anyone with reasonable grounds to suspect a child needs protection to report directly to a children's aid society. Tell your supervisor too, but you cannot hand the duty to someone else."),
("Which is a sign of possible physical abuse in an older adult?",
 "Bruises of different ages", ["Dry skin on the lower legs", "Thin and greying hair on the head", "Slower walking pace"],
 "Repeated injuries of different ages, or bruises in unusual places such as the upper arms, can point to abuse. Dry skin, greying hair and slower walking are normal ageing."),
("Which behaviour by a caregiver may suggest elder abuse at home?",
 "Not letting the client talk alone", ["Asking about side effects", "Keeping the home neat and tidy", "Attending the client's doctor visits"],
 "A caregiver who will not let the person talk alone with staff may be controlling or hiding abuse. Note it and report your observations to your supervisor."),
("When can a physical restraint be used in an Ontario long-term care home?",
 "Only as set out in the care plan", ["Whenever a resident is restless", "When staff are short on a shift", "As punishment for hitting staff"],
 "The Fixing Long-Term Care Act limits restraint to specific situations in the plan of care, ordered or approved by a physician or other authorized prescriber, with consent, monitoring and least-restraint alternatives tried first. Apart from an emergency under the common law duty to prevent serious harm, restraint for staff convenience or discipline is never allowed."),
("What does a \"least restraint\" approach mean?",
 "Try alternatives first", ["Use only soft restraints", "Restrain residents only at night", "Let family choose the restraint"],
 "Least restraint means using alternatives first, such as low beds, hip protectors, activities and more supervision, and restraining only when nothing else works and the law and plan allow."),
("A resident with a restraint in place should be released and repositioned how often, as a minimum?",
 "At least every 2 hours", ["At least every 8 hours", "At least once a shift", "Only when they ask"],
 "Ontario's long-term care regulation (O. Reg. 246/22) requires a resident restrained by a physical device to be released and repositioned at least once every two hours, and at any other time their condition calls for it. The resident is also monitored while restrained, as the regulation and the plan of care set out."),
("A PSW teases a resident about her weight in front of others. What is this?",
 "Emotional abuse", ["Friendly banter", "Physical abuse", "Financial abuse"],
 "Humiliating or ridiculing a person is emotional abuse, even if meant as a joke. It must be reported."),
("Which ethical principle means doing no harm?",
 "Non-maleficence", ["Beneficence", "Autonomy", "Distributive justice"],
 "Non-maleficence is avoiding harm. Beneficence is doing good, autonomy is respecting a person's own choices and justice is treating people fairly."),
("A client wants to keep smoking despite lung disease. Respecting this choice reflects which principle?",
 "Autonomy", ["Justice", "Fidelity", "Veracity"],
 "Autonomy is a capable person's right to make their own choices, including risky ones. You can share safety concerns and report them, but not override the choice."),
("Which Ontario law protects people from discrimination based on things like race, sex and disability?",
 "Human Rights Code", ["Health Care Consent Act", "Personal Health Information Protection Act", "Employment Standards Act"],
 "The Ontario Human Rights Code prohibits discrimination in services, housing and employment based on protected grounds, including race, sex, disability, age, religion and sexual orientation."),
("A resident and another resident, both capable, want to share a room as a couple. What should you do?",
 "Respect it and tell the team", ["Discourage it as unsuitable", "Ask their adult children to decide", "Report it as abuse"],
 "Capable adults have the right to intimacy and relationships. The Residents' Bill of Rights includes the right to share a room with another resident by mutual wish if accommodation is available."),
]
